# Contributing to Awesome-Lazy-Zsh

Thank you for considering a contribution. This document covers how to set up the project locally, how we use git-flow, and how to submit changes.

## Development Setup

```bash
# Clone your fork
git clone https://github.com/your-username/awesome-lazy-zsh.git
cd awesome-lazy-zsh

# Install dependencies
npm install

# Run tests
npm test

# Check syntax without running
node --check src/index.js
```

### Project Structure

```
src/
├── index.js              # Entry point, main flow orchestration
├── prompts.js            # Interactive CLI prompts
├── aliases/              # Zsh alias files (one per plugin category)
└── utils/
    ├── config.js         # Plugin/theme repository mappings
    ├── stateManager.js   # Resume setup state persistence
    ├── pluginManager.js  # Plugin selection and installation
    ├── themeManager.js   # Theme selection and application
    ├── serviceInstallFlow.js  # Service installation orchestration
    ├── serviceInstaller.js    # Platform-specific install commands
    ├── serviceRegistry.js     # Service metadata and binary mappings
    ├── platformDetector.js    # OS and package manager detection
    ├── zshrcManager.js        # .zshrc file generation
    ├── backupRestore.js       # Backup/restore functionality
    ├── profileManager.js      # Configuration profiles
    ├── updateManager.js       # Plugin update logic
    ├── customPlugins.js       # Custom plugin installation
    ├── commands.js            # Shell command execution
    └── systemIntegration.js   # OS-specific optimizations
tests/
├── *.test.js             # Unit tests (node:test)
└── *.property.test.js    # Property-based tests (fast-check)
```

### Tech Stack

- **Runtime**: Node.js (ES modules)
- **Dependencies**: `chalk` (styling), `prompts` (interactive input)
- **Test runner**: `node --test` (built-in)
- **Property testing**: `fast-check`
- **Shell**: Bash (`setup.sh`), Zsh (alias files)

## Git Flow Workflow

We use git-flow. All feature work starts from `develop` and merges back into it.

### Branches

| Branch | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| `master` | Production releases (tagged) |
| `develop` | Integration branch for next release |
| `feature/*` | New features |
| `release/*` | Release preparation |
| `hotfix/*` | Critical production fixes |

### Working on a Feature

```bash
git checkout develop
git pull origin develop
git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name

# Make changes, commit, push
git push -u origin feature/your-feature-name
```

Then open a pull request targeting `develop`.

### Commit Messages

Use conventional commits:

```
feat(plugins): add redis cluster aliases
fix(resume): handle corrupted state file gracefully
docs(readme): update plugin count
test(stateManager): add expiry boundary tests
chore(ci): update homebrew workflow
```

## Testing

Run the full test suite before submitting:

```bash
npm test
```

Tests run serially (`--test-concurrency=1`) because some tests share a state file path.

### Writing Tests

- Unit tests go in `tests/<module>.test.js`
- Property-based tests go in `tests/<module>.property.test.js`
- Use `node:test` and `node:assert/strict`
- Property tests use `fast-check` with a minimum of 100 iterations
- Tests that do file I/O should clean up in `beforeEach`/`afterEach`

## Adding a New Plugin

1. Add the entry to `src/utils/config.js`:
   - Built-in Oh My Zsh plugin: `'plugin-name': ''`
   - External plugin: `'plugin-name': 'https://github.com/...'`
   - Alias-only plugin: `'plugin-name': 'alias-only'`

2. If alias-only, create `src/aliases/plugin-name.zsh` with alias definitions.

3. If the plugin maps to an installable service, add it to `src/utils/serviceRegistry.js`.

4. Update the README plugin table.

## Adding a New Theme

1. Add the entry to `themeRepos` in `src/utils/config.js`:
   - Built-in: `'theme-name': ''`
   - External: `'theme-name': 'https://github.com/...'`

2. Update the README theme table.

## Pull Request Process

1. Target the `develop` branch.
2. Ensure `npm test` passes.
3. Describe what changed and why.
4. If adding a plugin or service, include the alias definitions and test coverage.
5. Keep PRs focused — one feature or fix per PR.

### PR Checklist

- [ ] Tests pass (`npm test`)
- [ ] No syntax errors (`node --check src/index.js`)
- [ ] README updated if user-facing behavior changed
- [ ] CHANGELOG updated with a brief entry
- [ ] Commit messages follow conventional format

## Reporting Issues

Include:
- Steps to reproduce
- Expected vs actual behavior
- OS, terminal emulator, and shell version
- Error output or logs (`setup.log`)

## Release Process

Handled by maintainers:

1. Create `release/vX.Y.Z` from `develop`
2. Bump version in `package.json`
3. Update CHANGELOG
4. Merge into `master`, tag, merge back into `develop`
5. Create GitHub release (triggers Homebrew formula update automatically)

## Code of Conduct

- Be respectful and constructive
- Focus on the code, not the person
- Help others learn

Thank you for contributing.
